:title("PHP (subset) interpreter") ;
:description("Parses PHP and executes it directly, without generating code.

A file starts in HTML MODE: text outside '<?php ... ?>' is literal output, '?>' also
stands in for a missing ';', the newline right after a close tag is swallowed and
'<?= e ?>' is shorthand for echo - so a template that interleaves markup with
foreach/endforeach runs as written. Covered:
dollar-sigil variables and variable variables, the full assignment set (= += -= *= /=
%= .= **= &= |= ^= <<= >>= ??=) and reference binding ($b = &$a), all the number
literal forms (decimal, 0x, 0b, 0o, legacy octal, underscores, floats with an
exponent, leading and trailing dot), single and double quoted strings with PHP's
escape set (including octal, hex and Unicode) and interpolation of '$var',
'$var[key]', '$var->prop' and '{$expr}', heredocs and nowdocs with terminator-indent
stripping, arrays as ordered hash maps with spreads, destructuring (nested, keyed
and with holes), isset/empty/unset, the nullsafe '?->' with argument short-circuit,
match expressions, closures with 'use' by value and by reference, arrow functions,
named arguments, variadics, first-class callable syntax, default parameter values,
by-reference parameters, foreach by value, by key and by reference, if/elseif/else,
while, do-while, for, foreach, try/catch/finally, throw as a statement and as an
expression, functions and classes with methods and properties, and echo/print.

The value model is PHP's own: int and float are distinct (3.0 === 3 is false), a PHP
array is ONE ordered key/value map (so === compares key order and == does not),
strings are byte strings (strlen counts UTF-8 bytes), '/' yields an int only when the
division is exact, '.' concatenates, and ==/===/<=>/< follow the PHP 8 comparison
rules (0 == \"a\" is false).

The int is 64 bit two's complement (PHP_INT_MAX is 9223372036854775807), and it does
NOT wrap on overflow: an arithmetic result that leaves 64 bits silently becomes a
float, so PHP_INT_MAX + 1 is 9.2233720368548E+18. The bitwise operators DO wrap, an
out-of-range float casts to int modulo 2^64, and an integer literal too large for 64
bits is a float. Floats have TWO renderers, which is PHP's own confusing pair: echo /
(string) / interpolation use precision=14, var_dump uses serialize_precision=-1 (the
shortest round-tripping digits) - so the same value prints as 9.2233720368548E+18 and
9.223372036854776E+18. Division by zero, modulo by zero and a negative shift count are
the catchable DivisionByZeroError / ArithmeticError PHP 8 made them.

Classes are covered in full: inheritance and interfaces, class constants (typed and
self-referential), static properties and methods, self:: / static:: / parent:: with
late static binding, constructor property promotion, anonymous classes, traits with
insteadof and as, enums (pure and backed, with cases()/from()/tryFrom()), the magic
methods (__get __set __isset __call __callStatic __invoke __toString __clone), and
Foo::class. Also covered: switch with fallthrough, do-while, the alternative
colon/endXXX control syntax, break N / continue N, goto within a statement list,
static local variables, global constants and the magic __FUNCTION__ / __CLASS__.

Generators are covered with one deliberate deviation: there are no coroutines in this
value model, so a generator's body runs to COMPLETION on the first iteration and the
yielded key/value pairs are then walked. Creation stays lazy and every side effect
happens exactly once, in order; what is lost is streaming, so an unbounded generator
does not terminate.

The program runs top to bottom; a self checking program ends with exit(failures).") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib): identifiers, integers, booleans.
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// PHP whitespace: blanks plus '#' and '//' line comments and '/* */' block comments.
Whitespace  = { @+" \t\r\n" | LineComment | BlockComment } ;
// '#' starts a line comment, but '#[' opens an ATTRIBUTE - the negative lookahead
// keeps the attribute out of the comment (without it '#[Foo] $p' swallows the rest
// of the line, which is how attributes used to "work" by accident).
LineComment = ( "//" | HashOnly ) :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HashOnly    = "#" !"[" ;
BlockComment = "/*" :whitespace() { NotStar | "*" { "*" } NotStarSlash } "*" { "*" } "/" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
NotNewline  = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStar     = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStarSlash = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\x2e" | "\x30"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// The whole program. A PHP file starts in HTML mode: everything before the first
// '<?php' is literal output, '?>' switches back to HTML mode, and the file may
// alternate any number of times (a file with no '<?php' at all is pure output).
Program     <~~ runProgram(takeAll()) ~~>
            = LeadHtml { TopItem } ;
// One item of a top level: a declaration or a statement. Factored out because a
// BRACED namespace body holds exactly the same items (see NsDecl).
TopItem     = Def <~~ push(makeDefStmt(pop())) ~~> | ClassDecl | EnumDecl | ConstDecl | TypeDecl | NsDecl | UseDecl | Statement ;
OpenTag     = "<?php" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// Text before the first open tag. Unlike the '?>' form it keeps a leading newline:
// only the newline directly after a CLOSE tag is swallowed.
LeadHtml    <~~ push(makeHtmlOut(pop(), false)) ~~>
            = HtmlText [ OpenTag ] ;
// '?>' leaves PHP mode. Everything up to the next '<?php' (or end of file) is echoed
// verbatim, and the newline immediately after '?>' is swallowed - PHP's own rule, so
// that a template line does not gain a blank line per tag. It is a STATEMENT, which
// is what makes the interleaved template form work inside a body:
//     <?php foreach ($rows as $r): ?><li><?php echo $r; ?></li><?php endforeach; ?>
// '?>' also stands in for a missing ';' (see Semi), exactly as in PHP.
InlineHtml  <~~ push(makeHtmlOut(pop(), true)) ~~>
            = "?>" HtmlText [ OpenTag ] ;
// The raw run itself. Whitespace skipping is turned OFF around the scan: the leading
// blanks and newlines of the run are OUTPUT, so they must not be eaten as separators
// before the token the scanner returns is matched.
HtmlText    <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = :whitespace() HtmlScan :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HtmlScan = :script(~~
    (function() {
        // Everything from here to the next opening tag is literal text. '<?php' is
        // matched case-insensitively (PHP's tag is), and '<?=' is the echo shorthand,
        // which ShortEcho then parses as an echo statement.
        var end = 0
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            var b = c.peek(end)
            if (b < 0) { break }
            if (b === 60 && c.peek(end + 1) === 63) {
                if (c.peek(end + 2) === 61) { break }
                var p1 = c.peek(end + 2)
                var h1 = c.peek(end + 3)
                var p2 = c.peek(end + 4)
                if ((p1 === 112 || p1 === 80) && (h1 === 104 || h1 === 72) && (p2 === 112 || p2 === 80)) { break }
            }
            end = end + 1
        }
        // An empty run must SUCCEED matching nothing; returning a zero-length token
        // would not. undefined is the dialect's "match empty".
        if (end === 0) { return undefined }
        // c.peek answers BYTES, so the UTF-8 has to be decoded here or a run holding
        // any non-ASCII text would never match back against the source.
        var out = ""
        var p = 0
        while (p < end) {
            var b0 = c.peek(p)
            if (b0 < 128) { out = out + String.fromCharCode(b0); p = p + 1; continue }
            if (b0 >= 240) {
                var cp4 = ((b0 - 240) * 262144) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 2) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 3) - 128)
                var off = cp4 - 65536
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(55296 + ((off - (off % 1024)) / 1024)) + String.fromCharCode(56320 + (off % 1024))
                p = p + 4
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 224) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 224) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 2) - 128))
                p = p + 3
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 192) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 192) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 1) - 128))
                p = p + 2
                continue
            }
            return abnf.newToken("\x01badutf8", 0)
        }
        return abnf.newToken(out, 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// '<?= e, e ?>' is shorthand for 'echo e, e;'.
ShortEcho   <~~ push(makeEcho(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ShortOpen <~~ push({cmd: "echo"}) ~~> [ Expression { "," Expression } ] [ ";" ] ;
ShortOpen   = "<?=" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A statement terminator. PHP lets a close tag stand in for the ';' ("echo 1 ?>"),
// so every statement that ends in ';' accepts a '?>' AHEAD of it instead - the tag
// itself is left in place and read as the InlineHtml statement that follows.
Semi        = ";" | CloseAhead ;
CloseAhead  = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i); continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc === 63 && c.peek(i + 1) === 62) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01noclosetag", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// A function definition: function name($a, $b) { ... } with explicit return. An
// optional parameter type hint and return type are accepted and ignored (dynamic).
Def         <~~ push(makeFun(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwFunction [ "&" ] Name <~~ push(up.in); genPush() ~~> ParamList [ ReturnType ] Block ;
ParamList   = "(" [ Param { "," Param } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
// A parameter accepts real-world surface: promotion modifiers (public/readonly...),
// a type hint, by-reference '&', variadic '...', and a default value. All but the
// name are accepted and dropped (dynamic, create-on-assign); the default expression
// is parsed then popped so only {param} reaches makeFun.
Param       <~~ push({param: makeParam(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ TypeHint ] [ PByRef ] [ PVariadic ] DollarName [ ParamDefault ] ;
PByRef      <~~ push({pbyref: true}) ~~> = "&" ;
PVariadic   <~~ push({pvariadic: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
ParamDefault <~~ push({pdefault: pop()}) ~~> = PlainEq Expression ;
TypeHint    = [ "?" ] TypeAtom { ( "|" | "&" ) [ "?" ] TypeAtom } ;
// A DNF type (PHP 8.2) parenthesizes an INTERSECTION inside a union: '(X&Y)|Z',
// 'X|(A&B)'. The parenthesized group is just another atom of the surrounding
// TypeHint, so one alternative covers every position a type may stand in (parameter,
// return, property). Type hints carry no runtime meaning in this subset.
TypeAtom    = KwArray | TypeParen | [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } ;
TypeParen   = "(" TypeHint ")" ;
ReturnType  = ":" TypeHint ;

// class C { public function m() {...} } -- method modifiers are accepted and ignored.
ClassDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { ClassMod } KwClass Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ KwExtends ExtBase ] [ KwImplements ImplList ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
ExtBase     <~~ push({ext: lastSeg(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = ExtName ;
ClassMod    = ( "abstract" | "final" | "readonly" ) KwEnd ;
ExtName     = [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } ;
ImplList    = ImplName { "," ImplName } ;
ImplName    <~~ push({impl: lastSeg(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = ExtName ;
Member      = MethodDecl | ClassConst | UseTrait | PropDecl ;
// A method: modifiers and a reference-return '&' are accepted and ignored; an
// abstract/interface method has no body (';' instead of a block -> empty method).
MethodDecl  <~~ push({method: makeFun(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } KwFunction [ "&" ] MethodName <~~ push(up.in); genPush() ~~> ParamList [ ReturnType ] ( Block | ";" ) ;
// Class constants: const A = 1, B = 2; with an optional TYPE before the first name
// (the typed class constants of PHP 8.3). The typed form has to be its own
// alternative and be tried FIRST - writing it as an optional [ TypeHint ] would let
// 'const SIZE = 4' consume SIZE as the type and then fail on '=', because a PEG
// never retries an optional that matched.
ClassConst  <~~ push({cconsts: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } KwConst ( ConstTyped | ConstPlain ) ";" ;
ConstTyped  = TypeHint ConstItem { "," ConstItem } ;
ConstPlain  = ConstItem { "," ConstItem } ;
ConstItem   <~~ push({cconst: makeConstRec(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = ConstName [ PlainEq Expression ] ;
ConstName   <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = Id ;
// use T1, T2; inside a class body - and, optionally, the conflict-resolution block
// PHP spells with 'insteadof' (which of two traits wins a name) and 'as' (bind a
// trait method under a second name, and/or change its visibility).
UseTrait    <~~ push({usetrait: makeUseTrait(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = KwUse ImplList ( ";" | TraitAdapt ) ;
TraitAdapt  = "{" { TraitRule } "}" ;
TraitRule   = TraitInsteadof | TraitAs ;
TraitInsteadof <~~ push({tinsteadof: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = TraitRef KwInsteadof ImplList ";" ;
TraitAs     <~~ push({tas: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = TraitRef KwAs [ TraitVis ] [ TraitAlias ] ";" ;
// The qualified form has to come first: 'T::m' starts exactly like the bare 'm'.
TraitRef    = TraitQual | TraitMeth ;
TraitQual   = TraitOwner "::" TraitMeth ;
TraitOwner  <~~ push({towner: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Name ;
TraitMeth   <~~ push({tmeth: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = MemberName ;
TraitVis    <~~ push({tvis: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" ) KwEnd ;
TraitAlias  <~~ push({talias: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = MemberName ;

// interface / trait / namespace / use-import: accepted structurally and routed to
// An interface is a named type with (abstract) method signatures: no bodies to run,
// but instanceof and the implements list need the name to exist at run time.
TypeDecl    = InterfaceDecl | TraitDecl ;
InterfaceDecl <~~ push(makeIfaceStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwInterface Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ KwExtends ImplList ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// A trait is a bag of members to be COPIED into a class (it has no instances and no
// place in the inheritance chain), so its descriptor just carries its parsed parts.
TraitDecl   <~~ push(makeTraitStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwTrait Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "{" { Member } "}" ;
// namespace N; and the BRACED form namespace N { ... } (and the global 'namespace {}').
// Names are not qualified in this subset, so the declaration itself stays a no-op -
// but the braced body is a top level of its own and its items are simply run in
// place, which is what makes a file that wraps everything in 'namespace X { }' work.
NsDecl      <~~ push(makeNs(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwNamespace { Id | "\\" } ( Semi | NsBody ) ;
NsBody      <~~ push({nsbody: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" { TopItem } "}" ;
// use A\B; / use A\B as C, D\E as F; / use function f; / use const C;
// and the GROUP form 'use Foo\Bar\{A, B as C, function f, const K,};' of PHP 7.
// Imports are not modelled in this subset, so the whole declaration is recognized
// structurally and routed to notImplemented - but it has to be recognized WHOLE, or
// the rest of the file is lost. UseGroup is tried first: its prefix ends in the '\{'
// that a plain path can never contain.
UseDecl     <~~ push(notImplStmt("use import", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwUse [ UseKind ] UseItem { "," UseItem } Semi ;
UseKind     = ( "function" | "const" ) KwEnd ;
UseItem     = UseGroup | UsePath ;
UseGroup    = [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } "\\" "{" UseInner { "," UseInner } [ "," ] "}" ;
UseInner    = [ UseKind ] UsePath ;
UsePath     = [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } [ KwAs Id ] ;
// enum Name : backing-type implements I { case A; ... }: recognized structurally and
// enum E: string implements I { case A = "a"; const C = 1; methods } - an enum is a
// class whose cases are SINGLETON instances, published as class constants (which is
// why E::A works with no extra machinery) and carrying ->name and, for a backed
// enum, ->value. EnumCase has to precede MethodDecl: both can start at a modifier.
EnumDecl    <~~ push(makeEnumStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwEnum Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" TypeAtom ] [ KwImplements ImplList ] "{" { EnumMember } "}" ;
EnumMember  = EnumCase | MethodDecl | ClassConst | UseTrait ;
EnumCase    <~~ push({ecase: makeConstRec(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = KwCase ConstName [ PlainEq Expression ] ";" ;
SkipDq      = '"' :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipDqChar } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipSq      = "'" :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipSqChar } "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipStrEsc  = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipDqChar  = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipSqChar  = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// A property declaration 'public $x;' or 'public int $x = e;' is accepted; a type
// hint and the initializer are dropped (properties are created on first assignment).
// One declaration may introduce SEVERAL properties - 'public $name, $bars;' and
// 'static public $a, $aa;' both occur in php-src's suite - so the tag pushes one
// {propdecl} per name rather than exactly one, and every name inherits the shared
// modifiers and type hint. makeClassStmt already walks the member list, so N records
// need no change there.
PropDecl    <~~ var pds = makeProps(takeAll()); for (var pi = 0; pi < pds.length; pi++) { push({propdecl: pds[pi]}) } ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ TypeHint ] DollarName [ "=" Expression ]
              { "," DollarName [ "=" Expression ] } ( ";" | PropHooks ) ;
// PHP 8.4 property hooks: 'public int $x = 1 { get => e; set { ... } }' (and the
// bodyless 'get;' / 'set;' of an abstract or interface property, which declares a hook
// without providing one). Each hook becomes an ordinary METHOD on the class -
// __hget_<prop> / __hset_<prop> - and a read or write of that property dispatches to
// it. The property itself stays a declared property and IS the backing store: inside
// its own hook '$this->x' reads and writes that store directly instead of re-entering
// the hook, which is PHP's rule and the only thing that keeps a backed hook from
// recursing forever. Mirrors php-to-llvm-ir.abnf member for member.
PropHooks   <~~ push({hooks: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" { PropHook } "}" ;
PropHook    <~~ push(makeHook(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ "&" ] HookName <~~ push({hname: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
              ( HookArrow | HookBlock | ";" ) ;
HookName    = ( "get" | "set" ) KwEnd ;
HookArrow   <~~ push({harrow: pop()}) ~~> = [ ParamList ] "=>" Expression ";" ;
HookBlock   <~~ push({hblock: pop().body}) ~~> = [ ParamList ] Block ;

// 'private(set)' / 'protected(set)' is PHP 8.4 asymmetric visibility: the setter is
// more restricted than the getter. Visibility is not enforced in this subset, so the
// '(set)' suffix is simply accepted (as php-to-llvm-ir.abnf already did).
Modifier    = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" | "static" | "final" | "abstract" | "readonly" ) KwEnd [ SetVis ] ;
SetVis      = "(" "set" ")" ;
// The modifier text is CAPTURED (as {mod}) rather than dropped: 'static' decides
// whether a property lives on the class or on the instance, and any modifier on a
// constructor parameter makes it a PROMOTED property.
PropMod     <~~ push({mod: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Modifier ;

// Body of a control structure / function: braces around a statement sequence.
Block       <~~ push({body: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "{" { Statement } "}" ;

// Every statement pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value | signal.
// A declaration is a statement too: PHP allows `if (...) { class Foo {} }` and a
// function declared inside a function body, and a conditionally declared class is
// common enough in real files to be worth parsing. Mirrors php-to-llvm-ir.abnf,
// whose Statement already carried them. DefStmt has to wrap Def so the thunk it
// pushes is the same makeDefStmt record TopItem builds; an ANONYMOUS function still
// rolls back here (Def demands a Name) and reaches ClosureExpr through ExprStmt.
Statement   = InlineHtml | ShortEcho | EchoStmt | UnsetStmt | GlobalStmt | StaticVarStmt | Block | If | While | ForEach | For
            | Switch | DoWhile | Try | Throw | Declare | Goto
            | Return | Break | Continue
            | DefStmt | ClassDecl | EnumDecl | TypeDecl | ConstDecl
            | Empty | LabelStmt | ExprStmt ;
DefStmt     <~~ push(makeDefStmt(pop())) ~~> = Def ;

// ----- widened statement surface -----
// switch, do-while, declare, goto and the static locals below are all genuinely
// modelled, as try/catch/finally and throw are.
// static $n = 0; inside a function: a variable whose STORAGE outlives the call. The
// cell is created on the first execution of this very statement and re-bound into
// every later frame, which is exactly PHP's per-declaration (not per-object) rule.
StaticVarStmt <~~ push(makeStaticVars(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwStatic DollarName [ PlainEq Expression ] { "," DollarName [ PlainEq Expression ] } Semi ;
// switch: loose (==) comparison, first match wins and execution FALLS THROUGH into
// the following clauses until a break. 'default' may sit anywhere, and is only taken
// when no case matched. The clause bodies are plain statement runs, which is why they
// are collected as lists rather than as Blocks - a Block would give each its own
// scope and would stop the fallthrough.
Switch      <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSwitch "(" Expression ")" ( SwitchAlt | SwitchBlock ) ;
SwitchBlock = "{" [ ";" ] { SwitchClause } "}" ;
SwitchAlt   = ":" [ ";" ] { SwitchClause } KwEndswitch Semi ;
SwitchClause = CaseClause | DefaultClause ;
CaseClause  <~~ push({scase: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = KwCase Expression CaseSep { Statement } ;
DefaultClause <~~ push({sdefault: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = KwDefault CaseSep { Statement } ;
CaseSep     = ":" !":" | ";" ;
DoWhile     <~~ push(makeDoWhile(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDo Body KwWhile "(" Expression ")" Semi ;
// goto L; / L: - a jump within one statement sequence (which is all PHP allows: it
// cannot enter a loop or leave a function). The jump travels as a control signal and
// is caught by the statement list that owns the label; see makeBody.
Goto        <~~ push(makeGoto(pop())) ~~>
            = KwGoto LabelName Semi ;
LabelStmt   <~~ push(makeLabel(pop())) ~~>
            = LabelName ":" !":" ;
LabelName   <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> = Name ;
// try/catch/finally + throw are genuinely modelled (excThrow/excTry, lib/interp-core.js):
// throw raises a value that unwinds to the nearest catch; catch binds it (the first catch
// clause wins - the exception type list, including multi-catch A | B, is parsed but not
// discriminated, and the catch variable is optional); finally always runs; a
// return/break/continue inside a try still propagates. A PHP Block is {body: thunk}, so
// the marked sub-rules unwrap it (pop().body). finBody passes the finally body's signal
// through: a normally-completing finally yields null (which excTry ignores), while a
// return/break/continue OUT OF the finally overrides the try/catch completion and cancels
// a pending rethrow - the same semantics js_try gives the compiler.
Try         <~~ push(excTryTyped(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwTry TryBody { CatchClause } [ FinallyClause ] ;
TryBody     <~~ push({trybody: pop().body}) ~~>
            = Block ;
CatchClause <~~ push(phpCatch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCatch "(" CatchType [ DollarName ] ")" Block <~~ push(pop().body) ~~> ;
FinallyClause <~~ push({finbody: finBody(pop().body)}) ~~>
            = KwFinally Block ;
Throw       <~~ push(excThrow(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow Expression Semi ;
// declare(strict_types=1); / declare(ticks=1) { ... }. None of PHP's three directives
// changes anything this subset models - there is no type coercion to tighten, no tick
// handler and no alternate source encoding - so the declaration itself is a no-op and
// the BRACED form simply runs its body where it stands. The one visible consequence:
// under strict_types real PHP raises a TypeError for a mismatched argument, and this
// subset does not check parameter types at all, so it accepts the call instead.
Declare     <~~ push(makeDeclare(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDeclare "(" DeclDirective { "," DeclDirective } [ "," ] ")" ( Semi | Block ) ;
DeclDirective <~~ takeAll() ~~> = Id "=" Expression ;
// const NAME = expr; at file level: a global constant. Unlike a class constant it
// takes no type, and its initializer is evaluated once, where the declaration stands.
ConstDecl   <~~ push(makeConstDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwConst ConstItem { "," ConstItem } Semi ;
CatchType   = CatchName { "|" CatchName } ;
CatchName   <~~ push({ctype: lastSeg(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = ExtName ;

// echo e, e, e;  /  print e;
EchoStmt    <~~ push(makeEcho(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwEcho <~~ push({cmd: up.in}) ~~> [ Expression { "," Expression } ] Semi ;

// global $a, $b; -- in this subset function bodies already resolve names through
// the enclosing (global) scope chain, so 'global' is accepted as a no-op.
GlobalStmt  <~~ takeAll(); push(makeConstStmt()) ~~>
            = KwGlobal DollarName { "," DollarName } Semi ;

Empty       <~~ push(makeConstStmt()) ~~>
            = ";" ;

// unset($a, $b): removes the bindings (a later isset($a) is false).
UnsetStmt   <~~ push(makeUnset(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwUnset "(" IsTarget { "," IsTarget } [ "," ] ")" Semi ;
IsTarget    <~~ push({istgt: pop()}) ~~>
            = Target ;

ExprStmt    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression Semi ;

// Both spellings of every compound statement: braces, and PHP's colon/endXXX form.
// The alternative form is tried FIRST because it is decided by a single ':' - it is
// AltBody that then has to be careful: a bare 'endif' would otherwise parse as a
// constant-reference statement and swallow the terminator, which is why the five
// endXXX words are reserved in NotKw.
// The body of every compound statement may be a BRACED BLOCK or a single unbraced
// statement - 'if ($i == 0) return;', 'foreach ($a as $v) echo $v;', 'while (0);'.
// Body keeps the two spellings behind one name and SingleStmt wraps the one statement
// in exactly the {body} record a Block pushes, so every builder below is reused
// unchanged. Block must be tried first (a '{' is both a block and a statement, and
// the two would produce the same tree, but the direct route is the cheap one).
//
// 'else if' needs no rule of its own once this exists: KwElseif matches only the
// single word 'elseif', so 'else if (...) {...}' takes the [ KwElse Body ] arm and
// Body parses the rest as a NESTED If - which is also exactly PHP's binding, with
// each 'else' attaching to the nearest open 'if'.
Body        = Block | SingleStmt ;
SingleStmt  <~~ push({body: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = Statement ;
If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf "(" Expression ")" ( AltIfTail | Body { ElseIf } [ KwElse Body <~~ push({els: pop().body}) ~~> ] ) ;
ElseIf      <~~ var b = pop(); push({elifCond: pop(), elifBody: b.body}) ~~>
            = KwElseif "(" Expression ")" Body ;
AltIfTail   = ":" AltBody { AltElseIf } [ KwElse ":" AltBody <~~ push({els: pop().body}) ~~> ] KwEndif Semi ;
AltElseIf   <~~ var b2 = pop(); push({elifCond: pop(), elifBody: b2.body}) ~~>
            = KwElseif "(" Expression ")" ":" AltBody ;
// A statement run that ends at the enclosing endXXX / else / elseif keyword. It
// pushes the same {body} record a Block does, so every builder is reused unchanged.
AltBody     <~~ push({body: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = { Statement } ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhile(pop().body, pop())) ~~>
            = KwWhile "(" Expression ")" ( AltWhileBody | Body ) ;
AltWhileBody = ":" AltBody KwEndwhile Semi ;

// for (init; cond; post) { body }. The three clauses are optional and wrapped
// with a kind marker so the builder can tell them apart on the stack.
For         <~~ push(makeFor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFor "(" [ ForInit ] ";" [ ForCond ] ";" [ ForPost ] ")" ( AltForBody | Body ) ;
AltForBody  = ":" AltBody KwEndfor Semi ;
ForInit     <~~ push({k: "init", t: exprToStmt(pop())}) ~~> = Expression ;
ForCond     <~~ push({k: "cond", t: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
ForPost     <~~ push({k: "post", t: exprToStmt(pop())}) ~~> = Expression ;

// foreach ($arr as $v) / foreach ($arr as $k => $v)
ForEach     <~~ push(makeForeach(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwForeach "(" Expression KwAs ForeachVars ")" ( AltForeachBody | Body ) ;
AltForeachBody = ":" AltBody KwEndforeach Semi ;
ForeachVars = DestructPat | ForeachKV ;
ForeachKV   = ForeachV1 [ "=>" ForeachV2 ] ;
ForeachV1   = [ FeRef ] DollarName <~~ push({v1: pop()}) ~~> ;
ForeachV2   = [ FeRef ] DollarName <~~ push({v2: pop()}) ~~> ;
// 'as &$v' aliases the array ELEMENT, so writing $v writes back into the array.
FeRef       <~~ push({byref: true}) ~~> = "&" ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwReturn [ Expression ] Semi ;
Break       <~~ push(makeBreak(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwBreak [ Expression ] Semi ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinue(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwContinue [ Expression ] Semi ;

// Every expression pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value.
Expression  = SuppressExpr | YieldExpr | RefAssign | RefAssignVal | DestructAssign | Assignment | Ternary ;
SuppressExpr = "@" Expression ;

// yield / yield $v / yield $k => $v / yield from $iterable. It is an EXPRESSION in
// PHP, and the presence of one anywhere in a body is what turns that function into a
// generator - which is why the tag marks the enclosing definition (genMark).
// 'yield from' must be tried first: 'from' would otherwise parse as a bare constant.
YieldExpr   <~~ push(makeYield(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwYield ( YieldFrom | [ YieldVal [ "=>" YieldVal ] ] ) ;
YieldFrom   <~~ push({yfrom: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwFrom Expression ;
YieldVal    <~~ push({yval: pop()}) ~~>
            = Ternary ;

// $b = &$a : both names then share ONE storage cell, so a write through either is
// visible through the other (PHP references, not a copy).
RefAssign   <~~ push(makeRefAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target PlainEq "&" !"&" Target ;
// '$h =& f();' - the right side is a call to a function DECLARED to return by
// reference ('function &f()'). Only a variable-like right side has a storage cell to
// share, so this form cannot go through makeRefAssign; it degrades to an ordinary
// assignment of the returned value, which is what the alias would have observed for
// every read. Tried after RefAssign, so '$b = &$a' keeps its exact real-reference
// binding.
RefAssignVal <~~ var rv = pop(); push(makeAssign([pop(), "=", rv])) ~~>
            = Target PlainEq "&" !"&" Expression ;

// list($a, $b) = e  /  [$a, $b] = e  -- positional destructuring of an indexed
// array into a list of assignment targets (tried before a plain expression so a
// bracketed target list is not mistaken for an array literal).
// list($a, $b) = e  /  [$a, $b] = e  -- array destructuring. A slot may be EMPTY
// ([, $second]), KEYED (["y" => $py]) or itself a nested pattern ([[$m, $n], [$o]]),
// and the same patterns bind a foreach value. Tried before a plain expression so a
// bracketed target list is not mistaken for an array literal; a real array literal
// still rolls back here because the '=' never follows.
DestructAssign <~~ push(makeDestructure(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DestructPat PlainEq Expression ;
DestructPat <~~ push({pat: makePattern(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = KwList "(" DSlots ")" | "[" DSlots "]" ;
DSlots      = DSlot { "," DSlot } ;
// Always pushes exactly one slot record, so an omitted element still occupies its
// position (that is what makes [, $second] bind the SECOND element).
DSlot       <~~ push(makeSlot(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ DElem ] ;
DElem       = DKeyed | DPlain ;
DKeyed      <~~ var v = pop(); push({dkey: pop(), dval: v}) ~~>
            = Expression "=>" DValue ;
DPlain      <~~ push({dval: pop()}) ~~>
            = DValue ;
DValue      = DestructPat | DTarget ;
// list(&$a, &$b) = $arr / foreach ($c as [&$x, $y]): a slot may bind BY REFERENCE.
// References into array storage are not modelled here, so the '&' is accepted and the
// slot binds by value - the syntax is what was blocking the file. The literal pushes
// nothing, so the rule stays item-neutral.
DTarget     <~~ push({dt: pop()}) ~~>
            = [ "&" !"&" ] Target ;

// cond ? a : b  /  cond ?: b (Elvis, yields cond when truthy) ; '??' null-coalescing
// binds tighter than the ternary but looser than ||.
Ternary     <~~ push(makeTernary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Coalesce [ Elvis | "?" Expression ":" Expression ] ;
Elvis       <~~ push({elvis: pop()}) ~~>
            = "?:" Expression ;
Coalesce    <~~ push(makeCoalesce(takeAll())) ~~>
            = OrExpr { "??" OrExpr } ;

Assignment  <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target AssignOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Expression ;
// Longest first (ordered choice): '**=' before '*=', '<<=' before '<='. The plain
// '=' declines '==' (a comparison) and '=>' (an array key), neither of which is an
// assignment - without the lookaheads it matches their first character and the rest
// of the sequence then fails somewhere else entirely.
AssignOp    = "**=" | "<<=" | ">>=" | "??=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | ".="
            | "&=" | "|=" | "^=" | PlainEq ;
PlainEq     = "=" !"=" !">" ;
Target      <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TargetHead { ArrowField | ArrowDyn | TIndex | TAppend } ;
// A static property is an assignable head too (self::$made = ..., C::$made++), so it
// sits alongside a plain variable. It must be tried FIRST: PlainHead would otherwise
// never see it, but VarVarHead / PlainHead both start at '$' and a bare class name
// is not a target at all, so the ordering only matters against a later ConstRef.
TargetHead  = StaticHead | PlainHead | VarVarHead ;
StaticHead  <~~ push({staticvar: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = StaticBase "::" StaticProp ;
PlainHead   = DollarName <~~ push({var: pop()}) ~~> ;
// $$name = e / ${expr} = e : the variable NAME is computed.
VarVarHead  <~~ push({varexpr: pop()}) ~~>
            = "$" ( VarRef | VarVar | "{" Expression "}" ) ;
ArrowField  = "->" MethodName <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> ;
// '$o->$name = e' and '$o->{expr} = e': the PROPERTY name is computed. It cannot be
// baked into the step record the way a literal name is, so the step carries a thunk
// and stepOf() materializes it - once per walk - into exactly the {key} record every
// reader below already understands. Tried after ArrowField, which cannot match a '$'
// or a '{' anyway.
ArrowDyn    <~~ push({keyT: pop()}) ~~>
            = "->" ( VarRef | "{" Expression "}" ) ;
TIndex      <~~ push({idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;
TAppend     <~~ push({append: true}) ~~>
            = "[" "]" ;

// The binary operator ladder, in PHP's own precedence order (loosest first):
//   ||  &&  |  ^  &  == != === !== <=>  < <= > >=  .  << >>  + -  * / %  **
// Note that since PHP 8 the concatenation '.' binds LOOSER than the shifts and the
// additive operators, which is why it has its own level rather than sitting next
// to '+'. The single-character bitwise operators decline their doubled forms
// ('&' !'&'), or 'a && b' parses as 'a & (&b)' - a wrong tree, not an error.
OrExpr      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = AndExpr { OrOp AndExpr } ;
OrOp        = "||" | ( "or" KwEnd ) ;
AndExpr     <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = PipeExpr { AndOp PipeExpr } ;
AndOp       = "&&" | ( "and" KwEnd ) ;
// '$x |> f(...)' is PHP 8.5's pipe: the left value becomes the single argument of the
// callable on the right, and the operator is LEFT associative, so '5 |> a |> b' is
// 'b(a(5))'. Its level is fixed by php-src's own test
//     assert(false && foo() . bar() |> baz() . quux());
// which means 'false && ((foo() . bar()) |> (baz() . quux()))' - tighter than '&&',
// looser than '.'. The right operand is any callable VALUE: a first-class callable
// 'f(...)', a closure, or a function name in a string ('5 |> "_test"' is in the suite
// too), which is what asCallable already normalizes for call_user_func.
PipeExpr    <~~ push(makePipe(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitOrExpr { "|>" BitOrExpr } ;
BitOrExpr   <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitXorExpr { SingleBar <~~ push(up.in) ~~> BitXorExpr } ;
SingleBar   = "|" !"|" !">" ;
BitXorExpr  <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitAndExpr { "^" <~~ push(up.in) ~~> BitAndExpr } ;
BitAndExpr  <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = EqExpr { SingleAmp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> EqExpr } ;
SingleAmp   = "&" !"&" ;
EqExpr      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RelExpr { ( "===" | "!==" | "==" | "!=" | "<=>" | "<>" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> RelExpr } ;
RelExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ConcatExpr { RelOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ConcatExpr } ;
RelOp       = LeNotSpaceship | ">=" | SingleLt | SingleGt ;
LeNotSpaceship = "<=" !">" ;
SingleLt    = "<" !"<" ;
SingleGt    = ">" !">" ;
ConcatExpr  <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ShiftExpr { DotOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ShiftExpr } ;
DotOp       = "." !"=" ;
ShiftExpr   <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AddExpr { ( "<<" | ">>" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> AddExpr } ;
AddExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = MulExpr { ( "+" | "-" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> MulExpr } ;
MulExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = UnaryExpr { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> UnaryExpr } ;

UnaryExpr   = Cast | Clone | InstanceofExpr | PreIncDec | Neg | Pos | BitNot | NotOp
            | Suppress | ThrowExpr | PowExpr ;
// '@expr' is PHP's error-suppression operator: it evaluates its operand and throws
// away any DIAGNOSTIC the evaluation would have produced. This subset does not model
// the diagnostic channel at all, so the operator is a pass-through and the rule is
// deliberately item-NEUTRAL (no tag) exactly like Pos - it pushes the one thunk its
// operand pushed. 19 files of php-src's suite open with '@foo()' or '@include $f'.
NotOp       <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "!" UnaryExpr ;
BitNot      <~~ push(makeBitNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "~" UnaryExpr ;
// '**' is RIGHT associative (2 ** 3 ** 2 is 512, not 64) and binds tighter than
// every prefix operator on its left but looser than the one on its right, so its
// right operand is a full UnaryExpr ('2 ** -1').
PowExpr     <~~ push(foldPow(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PostfixExpr [ "**" UnaryExpr ] ;
// (int)$x / (string)$y / (array)$z ...: a type cast. A parenthesized group '($x)'
// is not a cast (the inner is not a cast keyword) and falls through to Primary.
Cast        <~~ push(makeCast(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" CastKw <~~ push({cast: up.in}) ~~> ")" UnaryExpr ;
CastKw      = ( "integer" | "int" | "boolean" | "bool" | "float" | "double" | "real"
              | "string" | "array" | "object" | "unset" | "binary" ) KwEnd ;
// clone $o  /  clone($o)  and PHP 8.5's clone-WITH, 'clone($o, ["p" => 1])', which
// copies the object and then overwrites the named properties on the copy. CloneWith
// demands the comma, so an ordinary parenthesized 'clone($o)' rolls back into the
// UnaryExpr arm and keeps its exact previous parse.
Clone       = CloneWith | ClonePlain ;
ClonePlain  <~~ push(makeClone(pop())) ~~>
            = KwClone UnaryExpr ;
CloneWith   <~~ var w = pop(); push(makeCloneWith(pop(), w)) ~~>
            = KwClone "(" Expression "," Expression ")" ;
// throw is an EXPRESSION since PHP 8 ($x ?? throw new E, fn() => throw new E).
ThrowExpr   <~~ push(excThrow(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow Expression ;
// $x instanceof Foo : the runtime type test, matching the class itself, every base
// class and every implemented interface. Tried before the plain unary path; if no 'instanceof' follows, this alternative fails and rolls back, so an
// ordinary unary keeps its exact original parse (byte-identical).
InstanceofExpr <~~ push(makeInstanceof(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PostfixExpr KwInstanceof InstRef ;
InstRef     = InstDyn | InstName ;
InstDyn     <~~ push({idyn: pop()}) ~~> = VarRef ;
InstName    <~~ push({iname: up.in}) ~~> = ExtName ;
PreIncDec   <~~ push(makePreIncDec(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Target ;
Neg         <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>
            = "-" UnaryExpr ;
Pos         = "+" UnaryExpr ;
Suppress    = "@" UnaryExpr ;

PostfixExpr = IncDec | CallMember ;
IncDec      <~~ push(makeIncDec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;

// A primary followed by any mix of ->method(args), ->field, [index], (call), ::static.
CallMember  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Primary { MethodSfx | DynMethodSfx | FieldSfx | DynFieldSfx | IndexSfx | CallSfx | StaticSfx } ;
// '?->' short circuits: when the receiver is null the WHOLE remaining chain yields
// null and the arguments are never evaluated, so the two forms cannot share a rule.
MethodSfx   = NsMethodSfx | PlMethodSfx ;
PlMethodSfx <~~ push({kind: "mcall", ns: false, name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "->" MemberName <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ;
NsMethodSfx <~~ push({kind: "mcall", ns: true, name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "?->" MemberName <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ;
// $o->$meth(...) and $o->{"add"}(...): the method name is computed at run time.
DynMethodSfx <~~ push({kind: "dmcall", name: popDyn(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "->" DynName "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ;
DynName     <~~ push({dname: pop()}) ~~>
            = VarRef | "{" Expression "}" ;
FieldSfx    = NsFieldSfx | PlFieldSfx ;
PlFieldSfx  = "->" MemberName <~~ push({kind: "field", ns: false, name: up.in}) ~~> ;
NsFieldSfx  = "?->" MemberName <~~ push({kind: "field", ns: true, name: up.in}) ~~> ;
// '$o->$n' and '$o->{expr}': a property READ whose name is computed. The braced form
// has to be here as well as in DynMethodSfx, or '$o->{1234}' parses on the write side
// and not on the read side (DynMethodSfx only matches when a '(' follows).
DynFieldSfx <~~ push({kind: "dfield", name: pop()}) ~~>
            = "->" ( VarRef | "{" Expression "}" ) ;
IndexSfx    <~~ push({kind: "index", idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;
CallSfx     <~~ push({kind: "call", args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ;

// An argument list: positional, named (h: 5), spread (...$a) or the PHP 8.1
// first-class callable placeholder (strlen(...)), with an optional trailing comma.
ArgList     = Arg { "," Arg } [ "," ] ;
Arg         = NamedArg | SpreadArg | FccArg | LvalArg | PosArg ;
// An argument that is exactly a variable / element / property is ALSO remembered as
// an l-value, so a by-reference parameter (function f(&$n)) can bind the caller's
// storage cell. ArgEnd is the guard that keeps '$a + 1' out of this alternative:
// only a ',' or ')' may follow, otherwise the argument is an ordinary expression.
LvalArg     <~~ push(makeLvalArg(pop())) ~~>
            = Target ArgEnd ;
ArgEnd      = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i); continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc === 44 || cc === 41) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01notlval", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
NamedArg    <~~ push({argname: popLabel(), argval: pop()}) ~~>
            = ArgLabel Expression ;
ArgLabel    <~~ push({alabel: up.in}) ~~>
            = Id ":" !":" ;
SpreadArg   <~~ push({argspread: pop()}) ~~>
            = "..." Expression ;
FccArg      <~~ push({fcc: true}) ~~>
            = "..." ;
PosArg      <~~ push({argval: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
// '::' as a SUFFIX of a member chain: $obj::CONST, $obj::class, $obj::method().
// The class is taken from the value on the left (an instance, or a class name in a
// string), which is why makeStaticSfx receives the current chain value.
StaticSfx   <~~ push(makeStaticSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "::" StaticMember [ StaticArgs ] ;

Primary     = "(" Expression ")"
            | NewAnon | NewExpr | ClosureExpr | ArrowFn | Match | ArrayLit
            | IncludeExpr
            | HeredocLit | DqStr | SqStr | NumLit
            | True | False | Null
            | IssetExpr | EmptyExpr | VarVar | VarRef | StaticExpr | Call | ConstRef ;

// isset()/empty() are language constructs, not calls: isset() must NOT evaluate a
// missing variable into an error, and empty() applies PHP truthiness.
IssetExpr   <~~ push(makeIsset(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIsset "(" IsTarget { "," IsTarget } [ "," ] ")" ;
EmptyExpr   <~~ push(makeEmpty(pop())) ~~>
            = KwEmpty "(" Expression ")" ;
// $$name / ${expr}: a variable variable - the NAME is computed at run time.
VarVar      <~~ push(makeVarVar(pop())) ~~>
            = "$" ( VarRef | VarVar | "{" Expression "}" ) ;

NewExpr     <~~ push(makeNew(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew NewTarget [ "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ] ;
// new C(...) / new $className(...) / new static() - the class may be computed.
NewTarget   = NewStatic | NewDyn | NewParen | NewName ;
// new static() is LATE STATIC BINDING: it creates an instance of the class the call
// was made through, not of the one the code sits in. 'static' is a reserved word, so
// it cannot arrive through Name (self and parent can, and makeNew resolves all three
// through resolveClassName).
NewStatic   <~~ push({cls: "static"}) ~~> = "static" KwEnd ;
// The class of a 'new' may be NAMESPACE-QUALIFIED - 'new \ReflectionClass(...)' is
// the single commonest form in php-src's own suite. Names are not qualified in this
// subset, so only the last segment carries meaning, exactly as ConstRef does.
NewName     <~~ push({cls: lastSeg(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = QName ;
// new (expr)(args): the class is an arbitrary parenthesized expression (PHP 8).
NewParen    <~~ push({clsdyn: pop()}) ~~> = "(" Expression ")" ;
NewDyn      <~~ push({clsdyn: pop()}) ~~> = VarRef ;
// new class (args) extends B implements I { ... }: an anonymous class. The body is a
// real class body (Member), not a balanced skip, and the constructor arguments are
// labelled so makeNewAnon can tell them from the members.
NewAnon     <~~ push(makeNewAnon(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew [ AttrGrp ] KwClass [ AnonArgs ] [ KwExtends ExtBase ] [ KwImplements ImplList ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
AnonArgs    <~~ push({sargs: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ;

// fn (params) => expr : an arrow function (auto-capturing, single-expression), and
// match (subject) { arms }: a match expression. Both recognized and routed to
// fn (params) => expr : a single-expression closure that captures the enclosing
// variables its body mentions BY VALUE at creation time (changing $m afterwards
// cannot change what an already-created fn sees), which is what makeArrowFn snapshots.
ArrowFn     <~~ push(makeArrowFn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] [ "static" KwEnd ] KwFn [ "&" ] ParamList [ ReturnType ] "=>" ArrowBody ;
ArrowBody   <~~ push({body: arrowBody(pop())}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
// match (subject) { c1, c2 => e, default => e } : an EXPRESSION whose arms are
// compared with === (never with ==), first match wins, default may sit anywhere.
Match       <~~ push(makeMatch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwMatch "(" Expression ")" "{" [ MatchArm { "," MatchArm } [ "," ] ] "}" ;
MatchArm    = MatchDefault | MatchCase ;
MatchDefault <~~ push({marm: {conds: null, body: pop()}}) ~~>
            = KwDefault "=>" Expression ;
MatchCase   <~~ push({marm: makeArm(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = MatchCond { "," MatchCond } "=>" Expression ;
MatchCond   <~~ push({mcond: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;

// include / require (and _once): a file-inclusion expression. A single-quoted string
// literal operand ('geomlib.php') names a project file: its path is resolved and the
// file's top-level function / class declarations register globally (resolveImport /
// core.importFile, lib/interp-core.js). Any other operand - a dynamic expression, or a
// double-quoted string - is parsed and routed to notImplemented, exactly as before.
IncludeExpr <~~ push(makeInclude(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "include_once" | "include" | "require_once" | "require" ) KwEnd ( IncPath | IncDyn ) ;
// A single-quoted literal path, captured raw like SqStr but kept as the import path
// string (via up.in) rather than lowered to a runtime value.
IncPath     = "'" :whitespace() { SqEsc | SqPlain } <~~ push({incpath: unescapeSq(up.in)}) ~~> "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
IncDyn      <~~ push({incdyn: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
// A bareword constant reference: a global/class constant or magic constant (PHP_EOL,
// M_PI, __DIR__, __LINE__, ...). No constant model here, so it is routed to
// notImplemented. Placed last so calls, statics and keywords are matched first.
ConstRef    <~~ push(makeConstRef(lastSeg(stripWs(up.in)))) ~~>
            = QName ;
